{"exhaustive":{"nbHits":false,"typo":false},"exhaustiveNbHits":false,"exhaustiveTypo":false,"hits":[{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"dyscrete"},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai","agent"],"value":"Show HN: PhoneUse \u2013 Phones for <em>AI</em> <em>Agents</em>"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.phoneuse.com/"}},"_tags":["story","author_dyscrete","story_50347408","show_hn"],"author":"dyscrete","created_at":"2026-11-03T02:50:40Z","created_at_i":1793674240,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50347408","points":2,"story_id":50347408,"title":"Show HN: PhoneUse \u2013 Phones for AI Agents","updated_at":"2026-11-03T03:27:21Z","url":"https://www.phoneuse.com/"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"mxplusb"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai","agent"],"value":"My cofounders and I are in the process of spinning up an <em>AI</em> integration lab, and over the last year we've consumed billions of tokens across multiple American, European, and Chinese models as a force multiplier for building out our products. However, despite our extensive technical expertise, we've still discovered there are long-term ramifications to heavy <em>agent</em>ic <em>AI</em> usage. So I decided to run an experiment for us: given a specification, can an LLM fully, concretely, and correctly implement an internally consistent, linearizable distributed system to the same quality as a human?<p>This repository is the outcome and showcase of that experiment, complete with the entire session history, including tool outputs. To make this project, I only gave the LLM the AMQP 0.9.1 specification and a few requirements: it must use clustered Raft, and it must support up to 9 nodes (light ceiling, but not the final limit). I picked an older but less well-known protocol as it provided a simplistic base with a wide range of features built on top; a messaging platform more than just a broker. While the specification itself is fairly old, it is still very viable and valuable today for a wide range of uses.<p>For our uses, I chose Switchboard as the experiment as our products are sealed appliances and we need a stable, scalable message broker that's embeddable into our existing apps (embedded distributed systems are easier when it's all the same binary), deployable as a standalone service, and understandable enough by everyone that debugging and diagnosing it is easy. This experiment is not designed to compete with RabbitMQ or other solutions (altho it is a love letter to my former vmware/Pivotal coworkers who worked on RabbitMQ), and I will be maintaining it for our own use cases.<p>I wrote it in Rust specifically because our code base is written in Rust. While I may talk about the hands-on design, build, and testing of the project as &quot;automated&quot; and &quot;with long horizon capabilities&quot;, it is not vibe-coded slop. There are hundreds of cross-platform and cross-architecture tests, and full Jepsen linearization tests on Linux - I am going to put this software into high-compliance production environments and I need it to be stable.<p>In finality, I hope you find my little experiment in automated, spec-driven, long-horizon builds interesting and insightful. Contributions are welcome, if desired, but with the age of the protocol I don't expect any real consumers other than our lab.<p>Happy message passing!"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: An experiment in automated long-horizon builds with AMQP 0.9.1"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://github.com/siennathesane/switchboard"}},"_tags":["story","author_mxplusb","story_50342493","show_hn"],"author":"mxplusb","children":[50346664,50342544],"created_at":"2026-11-02T19:40:03Z","created_at_i":1793648403,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50342493","points":1,"story_id":50342493,"story_text":"My cofounders and I are in the process of spinning up an AI integration lab, and over the last year we&#x27;ve consumed billions of tokens across multiple American, European, and Chinese models as a force multiplier for building out our products. However, despite our extensive technical expertise, we&#x27;ve still discovered there are long-term ramifications to heavy agentic AI usage. So I decided to run an experiment for us: given a specification, can an LLM fully, concretely, and correctly implement an internally consistent, linearizable distributed system to the same quality as a human?<p>This repository is the outcome and showcase of that experiment, complete with the entire session history, including tool outputs. To make this project, I only gave the LLM the AMQP 0.9.1 specification and a few requirements: it must use clustered Raft, and it must support up to 9 nodes (light ceiling, but not the final limit). I picked an older but less well-known protocol as it provided a simplistic base with a wide range of features built on top; a messaging platform more than just a broker. While the specification itself is fairly old, it is still very viable and valuable today for a wide range of uses.<p>For our uses, I chose Switchboard as the experiment as our products are sealed appliances and we need a stable, scalable message broker that&#x27;s embeddable into our existing apps (embedded distributed systems are easier when it&#x27;s all the same binary), deployable as a standalone service, and understandable enough by everyone that debugging and diagnosing it is easy. This experiment is not designed to compete with RabbitMQ or other solutions (altho it is a love letter to my former vmware&#x2F;Pivotal coworkers who worked on RabbitMQ), and I will be maintaining it for our own use cases.<p>I wrote it in Rust specifically because our code base is written in Rust. While I may talk about the hands-on design, build, and testing of the project as &quot;automated&quot; and &quot;with long horizon capabilities&quot;, it is not vibe-coded slop. There are hundreds of cross-platform and cross-architecture tests, and full Jepsen linearization tests on Linux - I am going to put this software into high-compliance production environments and I need it to be stable.<p>In finality, I hope you find my little experiment in automated, spec-driven, long-horizon builds interesting and insightful. Contributions are welcome, if desired, but with the age of the protocol I don&#x27;t expect any real consumers other than our lab.<p>Happy message passing!","title":"Show HN: An experiment in automated long-horizon builds with AMQP 0.9.1","updated_at":"2026-11-03T01:09:28Z","url":"https://github.com/siennathesane/switchboard"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"laotoutou"},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai","agent"],"value":"Show HN: A persistent Chrome cloud browser for <em>AI</em> <em>agents</em>"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://cloudbrowser.tv"}},"_tags":["story","author_laotoutou","story_50340718","show_hn"],"author":"laotoutou","children":[50340886],"created_at":"2026-11-02T17:53:47Z","created_at_i":1793642027,"num_comments":1,"objectID":"50340718","points":1,"story_id":50340718,"title":"Show HN: A persistent Chrome cloud browser for AI agents","updated_at":"2026-11-02T18:05:29Z","url":"https://cloudbrowser.tv"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"AylonArmstrong"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai","agent"],"value":"Hi HN,<p>My name is Aylon and I'm building ArchDocs. I started building ArchDocs in late 2024 as an architecture diagramming tool with reusable references and objects (still exists today and is usable).<p>As I've been using <em>AI</em> to code more and more, one of the things I've run into is that my <em>agent</em> doesn't have enough context around past decisions or findings that could affect an implementation, causing some nasty side-effects like redundant investigations, incorrect assumptions, repeating previously caught mistakes and ultimately wasting precious tokens.<p>Let's say for example, you need to do a migration that affects 10 backend services. Your <em>agent</em> starts working on the first one and during the implementation and verification process, it discovers an issue that will undeniably affect the other 9 services. The next <em>agent</em> can start on the second service without knowing what was learned on the first.<p>There's another problem I run up against time and time again. I need a dependable way to verify and log what an <em>agent</em> has done, the outcome of that and any deviations or issues that arise from it, so I can tell whether the change actually did what I intended.<p>Enter Design Docs inside ArchDocs.<p>The workflow is simple. You or your <em>agent</em> can craft a spec and ask questions for people to answer. Writing the implementation plan comes next, followed by doing the actual work. While you or your <em>agent</em> works, you track important milestones, deviations, decisions and more in the work log. Need to pause or have someone else do the work? No problem, craft a handoff or let ArchDocs <em>AI</em> do it for you. Finally, you or your <em>agent</em> logs a summarized version of what was done and how it was verified in an evidence of work submission.<p>In that migration example, your <em>agent</em> can mark the issue in the work log as a &quot;finding&quot; and that will then be surfaced when future <em>agents</em> execute on the other 9 services.<p>On the verification side, you may ask, &quot;How does this help if the <em>agent</em> or engineer is just writing down their verification?&quot; This is a good question and that's only one part of the verification. We have this concept of <em>Agent</em> attested (paired with human verified) and human attested. The last pillar and the most important is ArchDocs' own independent, automated verifications.<p>Right now, we just have GitHub tracking for check results but we will soon be expanding this into so much more.<p>Imagine one of the automated verifications is that a specific log with a specific attribute shows up in Datadog. Maybe even a specific event showing up in PostHog. There's a lot of ways we're going to take this and we're excited to share them once done.<p>You can explore an example design doc without signing up. It includes the spec, implementation plan, work log and verification evidence:<p><a href=\"https://archdocs.io/templates/design-doc-example\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://archdocs.io/templates/design-doc-example</a>"},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai","agent"],"value":"Show HN: ArchDocs \u2013 Shared context and verification for engineers and <em>AI</em> <em>agents</em>"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://archdocs.io"}},"_tags":["story","author_AylonArmstrong","story_50340570","show_hn"],"author":"AylonArmstrong","created_at":"2026-11-02T17:44:32Z","created_at_i":1793641472,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50340570","points":2,"story_id":50340570,"story_text":"Hi HN,<p>My name is Aylon and I&#x27;m building ArchDocs. I started building ArchDocs in late 2024 as an architecture diagramming tool with reusable references and objects (still exists today and is usable).<p>As I&#x27;ve been using AI to code more and more, one of the things I&#x27;ve run into is that my agent doesn&#x27;t have enough context around past decisions or findings that could affect an implementation, causing some nasty side-effects like redundant investigations, incorrect assumptions, repeating previously caught mistakes and ultimately wasting precious tokens.<p>Let&#x27;s say for example, you need to do a migration that affects 10 backend services. Your agent starts working on the first one and during the implementation and verification process, it discovers an issue that will undeniably affect the other 9 services. The next agent can start on the second service without knowing what was learned on the first.<p>There&#x27;s another problem I run up against time and time again. I need a dependable way to verify and log what an agent has done, the outcome of that and any deviations or issues that arise from it, so I can tell whether the change actually did what I intended.<p>Enter Design Docs inside ArchDocs.<p>The workflow is simple. You or your agent can craft a spec and ask questions for people to answer. Writing the implementation plan comes next, followed by doing the actual work. While you or your agent works, you track important milestones, deviations, decisions and more in the work log. Need to pause or have someone else do the work? No problem, craft a handoff or let ArchDocs AI do it for you. Finally, you or your agent logs a summarized version of what was done and how it was verified in an evidence of work submission.<p>In that migration example, your agent can mark the issue in the work log as a &quot;finding&quot; and that will then be surfaced when future agents execute on the other 9 services.<p>On the verification side, you may ask, &quot;How does this help if the agent or engineer is just writing down their verification?&quot; This is a good question and that&#x27;s only one part of the verification. We have this concept of Agent attested (paired with human verified) and human attested. The last pillar and the most important is ArchDocs&#x27; own independent, automated verifications.<p>Right now, we just have GitHub tracking for check results but we will soon be expanding this into so much more.<p>Imagine one of the automated verifications is that a specific log with a specific attribute shows up in Datadog. Maybe even a specific event showing up in PostHog. There&#x27;s a lot of ways we&#x27;re going to take this and we&#x27;re excited to share them once done.<p>You can explore an example design doc without signing up. It includes the spec, implementation plan, work log and verification evidence:<p><a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;archdocs.io&#x2F;templates&#x2F;design-doc-example\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;archdocs.io&#x2F;templates&#x2F;design-doc-example</a>","title":"Show HN: ArchDocs \u2013 Shared context and verification for engineers and AI agents","updated_at":"2026-11-02T18:05:29Z","url":"https://archdocs.io"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"bshivarthy"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai","agent"],"value":"Back in 2022 I was building a data and news site for family offices, private wealth and investment managers. We used the early OpenAI models to write data-driven stories covering the markets for art, collectibles, luxury goods and other cultural assets.<p>Mid-2024, a well-known <em>AI</em> company asked to license our stories. The big labs were signing licensing deals with media companies.<p>This is when it clicked. The biggest reader of news wasn\u2019t going to be people. It was going to be their agents, and agents have unlimited hours of attention.<p>So I built Synorb. It reads thousands of sources and turns fresh content objects into Manifests (structured claims with provenance) that are dispatched via Streams anchored to organizations, people and datasets.<p>Hanging Context is an experiment on top of that. Could an <em>agent</em> read our streams and stand up a news site for other agents to read and find trustworthy?<p>The first version with a home-brewed Hermes <em>agent</em> was cumbersome so I tried Instinct and Muse when they released and settled on Muse as I found it better at narratives, images.<p>An <em>agent</em> runs the site. I wrote this post.<p>How it works:<p>Every 12 hours Muse reads hundreds of new Manifests published in our streams, then writes a new edition for the site. Every article produced cites the Manifests it used.<p>Before it publishes an article, an API checks it for quotes. They must appear word for word in a cited Manifest. Every paragraph needs to be backed by a citation and sources have to be under 24 hours old.<p>Muse doesn\u2019t probabilistically determine what to trust - if 3 or more different sources back it, it\u2019s &quot;Confirmed&quot;. Otherwise it\u2019s &quot;Reported&quot;. At the time of writing, the site had 30 of 45 articles Confirmed. Here\u2019s an article with 3 sources cited; the panel at the bottom shows what was checked: <a href=\"https://hangingcontext.com/stories/2026-11-02/agent-stack-rebuilt-from-below/\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://hangingcontext.com/stories/2026-11-02/<em>agent</em>-stack-re...</a><p>It costs almost nothing to run as I'm using Muse on the free plan, and since it only needs to pull Manifests, reason and write, it can run just fine.<p>To try it, just add <a href=\"https://hangingcontext.com/mcp\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://hangingcontext.com/mcp</a> to Claude, Cursor or any MCP client. No sign-up or key required. Setup: <a href=\"https://github.com/Synorb/hanging-context\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/Synorb/hanging-context</a>.<p>What I\u2019m trying to figure out:<p>When I saw the early pull numbers by agents, I thought this could be a lead-gen micro-site for Synorb. I\u2019ve built those before for human focused consumer and enterprise products.<p>Conversion is now the challenge. For a casual daily read, Hanging Context already does all the work. I don\u2019t want to be annoying and cut coverage or show previews. So my plan is to just keep it free and try grow the number of agents pulling daily.<p>Full Disclosure: Synorb is my paid product, and Muse reads through its own Synorb account.<p>Curious if your agents would trust news written by another <em>agent</em> and what I can do to improve the odds."},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"partial","matchedWords":["agent"],"value":"Show HN: Hanging Context \u2013 news by an <em>agent</em>, for other agents"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://hangingcontext.com/"}},"_tags":["story","author_bshivarthy","story_50340083","show_hn"],"author":"bshivarthy","created_at":"2026-11-02T17:16:01Z","created_at_i":1793639761,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50340083","points":1,"story_id":50340083,"story_text":"Back in 2022 I was building a data and news site for family offices, private wealth and investment managers. We used the early OpenAI models to write data-driven stories covering the markets for art, collectibles, luxury goods and other cultural assets.<p>Mid-2024, a well-known AI company asked to license our stories. The big labs were signing licensing deals with media companies.<p>This is when it clicked. The biggest reader of news wasn\u2019t going to be people. It was going to be their agents, and agents have unlimited hours of attention.<p>So I built Synorb. It reads thousands of sources and turns fresh content objects into Manifests (structured claims with provenance) that are dispatched via Streams anchored to organizations, people and datasets.<p>Hanging Context is an experiment on top of that. Could an agent read our streams and stand up a news site for other agents to read and find trustworthy?<p>The first version with a home-brewed Hermes agent was cumbersome so I tried Instinct and Muse when they released and settled on Muse as I found it better at narratives, images.<p>An agent runs the site. I wrote this post.<p>How it works:<p>Every 12 hours Muse reads hundreds of new Manifests published in our streams, then writes a new edition for the site. Every article produced cites the Manifests it used.<p>Before it publishes an article, an API checks it for quotes. They must appear word for word in a cited Manifest. Every paragraph needs to be backed by a citation and sources have to be under 24 hours old.<p>Muse doesn\u2019t probabilistically determine what to trust - if 3 or more different sources back it, it\u2019s &quot;Confirmed&quot;. Otherwise it\u2019s &quot;Reported&quot;. At the time of writing, the site had 30 of 45 articles Confirmed. Here\u2019s an article with 3 sources cited; the panel at the bottom shows what was checked: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;hangingcontext.com&#x2F;stories&#x2F;2026-11-02&#x2F;agent-stack-rebuilt-from-below&#x2F;\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;hangingcontext.com&#x2F;stories&#x2F;2026-11-02&#x2F;agent-stack-re...</a><p>It costs almost nothing to run as I&#x27;m using Muse on the free plan, and since it only needs to pull Manifests, reason and write, it can run just fine.<p>To try it, just add <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;hangingcontext.com&#x2F;mcp\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;hangingcontext.com&#x2F;mcp</a> to Claude, Cursor or any MCP client. No sign-up or key required. Setup: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;Synorb&#x2F;hanging-context\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;Synorb&#x2F;hanging-context</a>.<p>What I\u2019m trying to figure out:<p>When I saw the early pull numbers by agents, I thought this could be a lead-gen micro-site for Synorb. I\u2019ve built those before for human focused consumer and enterprise products.<p>Conversion is now the challenge. For a casual daily read, Hanging Context already does all the work. I don\u2019t want to be annoying and cut coverage or show previews. So my plan is to just keep it free and try grow the number of agents pulling daily.<p>Full Disclosure: Synorb is my paid product, and Muse reads through its own Synorb account.<p>Curious if your agents would trust news written by another agent and what I can do to improve the odds.","title":"Show HN: Hanging Context \u2013 news by an agent, for other agents","updated_at":"2026-11-02T17:20:44Z","url":"https://hangingcontext.com/"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"aming557"},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai","agent"],"value":"Show HN: <em>Agent</em> Drift Game \u2013 short games about why <em>AI</em> agents drift"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://drift.seethis.dev/"}},"_tags":["story","author_aming557","story_50339784","show_hn"],"author":"aming557","children":[50339808],"created_at":"2026-11-02T16:58:12Z","created_at_i":1793638692,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50339784","points":1,"story_id":50339784,"title":"Show HN: Agent Drift Game \u2013 short games about why AI agents drift","updated_at":"2026-11-02T17:00:28Z","url":"https://drift.seethis.dev/"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"esamust"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai","agent"],"value":"If you want to see what org MMM itself uses: <a href=\"https://mindmademany.com/mmm\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://mindmademany.com/mmm</a><p>I created this platform because I wanted to have a system that allows our different <em>AI</em> subscriptions to organize and talk to each other as well as keep the roles separate from the providers and their chats. Basically have our <em>agents</em> independent from a specific chats and platforms and swapable at any time without having to &quot;onboard&quot; the role again. This also allows the <em>agents</em> to do long term tasks beyond the individual models trained window and for me most of the time only needing to talk to the director <em>agent</em>.<p>It started out just wanting to keep my <em>agents</em> within their own role to keep them always sharp for their specific task and mixing models from Claude and OpenAI to minimize blind spots. A team chat app is not enough because it makes visually very hard to connect and track <em>agents</em> in more complicated ways. So I build this to do all of that.<p>If you were to ask me why use this instead of paperclip for example, the answer I'm not really sure because I don't know much about paperclip. I just build this because I knew exactly the use case for us, wanted my organization to always be available and organizable by the <em>agents</em> themselves with their role context, history, decisions, etc. always available.<p>I use the MMM organization shown in <a href=\"https://mindmademany.com/mmm\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://mindmademany.com/mmm</a> to develop the tool itself which means the <em>agents</em> themselves improve the tool when they see issues and improvements with any parts of the platform.<p>The platform has a free tier up to 8 filled roles after which more roles are behind a subscription. There's a ton of ways to make this more efficient but to keep things under control at the beginning the subscription tiers are set up as is. We want to avoid monetizing being the middleman for tokens since everybody has their favorite <em>AI</em> subs anyway and prefer to chat in them."},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"partial","matchedWords":["agent"],"value":"Show HN: Mind Made Many \u2013 Platform to organize your <em>agents</em> into orgs"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://mindmademany.com/"}},"_tags":["story","author_esamust","story_50339212","show_hn"],"author":"esamust","created_at":"2026-11-02T16:25:00Z","created_at_i":1793636700,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50339212","points":2,"story_id":50339212,"story_text":"If you want to see what org MMM itself uses: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;mindmademany.com&#x2F;mmm\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;mindmademany.com&#x2F;mmm</a><p>I created this platform because I wanted to have a system that allows our different AI subscriptions to organize and talk to each other as well as keep the roles separate from the providers and their chats. Basically have our agents independent from a specific chats and platforms and swapable at any time without having to &quot;onboard&quot; the role again. This also allows the agents to do long term tasks beyond the individual models trained window and for me most of the time only needing to talk to the director agent.<p>It started out just wanting to keep my agents within their own role to keep them always sharp for their specific task and mixing models from Claude and OpenAI to minimize blind spots. A team chat app is not enough because it makes visually very hard to connect and track agents in more complicated ways. So I build this to do all of that.<p>If you were to ask me why use this instead of paperclip for example, the answer I&#x27;m not really sure because I don&#x27;t know much about paperclip. I just build this because I knew exactly the use case for us, wanted my organization to always be available and organizable by the agents themselves with their role context, history, decisions, etc. always available.<p>I use the MMM organization shown in <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;mindmademany.com&#x2F;mmm\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;mindmademany.com&#x2F;mmm</a> to develop the tool itself which means the agents themselves improve the tool when they see issues and improvements with any parts of the platform.<p>The platform has a free tier up to 8 filled roles after which more roles are behind a subscription. There&#x27;s a ton of ways to make this more efficient but to keep things under control at the beginning the subscription tiers are set up as is. We want to avoid monetizing being the middleman for tokens since everybody has their favorite AI subs anyway and prefer to chat in them.","title":"Show HN: Mind Made Many \u2013 Platform to organize your agents into orgs","updated_at":"2026-11-02T17:05:43Z","url":"https://mindmademany.com/"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"xescure"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai","agent"],"value":"...the first publicly available node software for Keeta. Created with heavy use of <em>AI</em> <em>agents</em>. Not affiliated with Keeta.<p>You can run a replica, a peering node or a (testnet) rep with Docker in a few lines. Mine is running at <a href=\"https://node.xescu.re/api/node/version\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://node.xescu.re/api/node/version</a><p>The true digitalization of finance, especially in cross-border contexts, has stalled. Blockchain technology may bring about a new chapter where money is as fast as the internet is. However, current blockchains are unscalable and clunky and I don't see them being the next financial backbone at all.<p>Keeta is insanely fast. (Google measured 11 million transactions per second: <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/financial-services/how-blockchain-network-keeta-processes-11-million-transactions-per-second-with-spanner\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/financial-services/how-...</a>) Settlement is sub-second and deterministic. Keeta's mental model maps cleanly to payments, and it doesn't try to force smart contracts where they're not needed. Tokens can be equipped with KYC/AML/sanctions controls for assets and jurisdictions that so require. Ramps, bridges, and liquidity providers are not an afterthought, but native first-class participants of the network. Using these components, for the first time in history, a solo developer with an <em>AI</em> <em>agent</em> can set up an entire neobank in just minutes. I believe Keeta will power a new wave of financial apps, where the best user experience, security, and feature set will win. Builders can focus on building, and the open and interoperable network takes care of providing them with the best infrastructure, instead of depending on closed-room financial deals.<p>I can't wait to see how Keeta develops, but right now its source code isn't fully public and third parties are not able to run their own nodes. I fed Claude Opus 5.5 the public parts of the code, my understanding of Keeta, its docs and forum discussions. Then came about 6 hours of refinement and setting up an environment where the <em>agent</em> could evaluate itself. Finally, it ran for a few days autonomously:<p>The end result is a fully functional Keeta node. It's primarily for experimentation, please don't rely on it for high value applications (yet?)."},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: Slop Node for Keeta"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://github.com/xescure/slop-node"}},"_tags":["story","author_xescure","story_50337688","show_hn"],"author":"xescure","created_at":"2026-11-02T14:58:19Z","created_at_i":1793631499,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50337688","points":2,"story_id":50337688,"story_text":"...the first publicly available node software for Keeta. Created with heavy use of AI agents. Not affiliated with Keeta.<p>You can run a replica, a peering node or a (testnet) rep with Docker in a few lines. Mine is running at <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;node.xescu.re&#x2F;api&#x2F;node&#x2F;version\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;node.xescu.re&#x2F;api&#x2F;node&#x2F;version</a><p>The true digitalization of finance, especially in cross-border contexts, has stalled. Blockchain technology may bring about a new chapter where money is as fast as the internet is. However, current blockchains are unscalable and clunky and I don&#x27;t see them being the next financial backbone at all.<p>Keeta is insanely fast. (Google measured 11 million transactions per second: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;cloud.google.com&#x2F;blog&#x2F;topics&#x2F;financial-services&#x2F;how-blockchain-network-keeta-processes-11-million-transactions-per-second-with-spanner\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;cloud.google.com&#x2F;blog&#x2F;topics&#x2F;financial-services&#x2F;how-...</a>) Settlement is sub-second and deterministic. Keeta&#x27;s mental model maps cleanly to payments, and it doesn&#x27;t try to force smart contracts where they&#x27;re not needed. Tokens can be equipped with KYC&#x2F;AML&#x2F;sanctions controls for assets and jurisdictions that so require. Ramps, bridges, and liquidity providers are not an afterthought, but native first-class participants of the network. Using these components, for the first time in history, a solo developer with an AI agent can set up an entire neobank in just minutes. I believe Keeta will power a new wave of financial apps, where the best user experience, security, and feature set will win. Builders can focus on building, and the open and interoperable network takes care of providing them with the best infrastructure, instead of depending on closed-room financial deals.<p>I can&#x27;t wait to see how Keeta develops, but right now its source code isn&#x27;t fully public and third parties are not able to run their own nodes. I fed Claude Opus 5.5 the public parts of the code, my understanding of Keeta, its docs and forum discussions. Then came about 6 hours of refinement and setting up an environment where the agent could evaluate itself. Finally, it ran for a few days autonomously:<p>The end result is a fully functional Keeta node. It&#x27;s primarily for experimentation, please don&#x27;t rely on it for high value applications (yet?).","title":"Show HN: Slop Node for Keeta","updated_at":"2026-11-02T15:06:12Z","url":"https://github.com/xescure/slop-node"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"bshafiq"},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai","agent"],"value":"Show HN: <em>Agent</em>TrafficCop \u2013 Stop <em>AI</em> <em>agent</em>s from looping and burning money"},"url":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"partial","matchedWords":["agent"],"value":"https://<em>agent</em>trafficcop.com"}},"_tags":["story","author_bshafiq","story_50337488","show_hn"],"author":"bshafiq","children":[50337505],"created_at":"2026-11-02T14:43:09Z","created_at_i":1793630589,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50337488","points":4,"story_id":50337488,"title":"Show HN: AgentTrafficCop \u2013 Stop AI agents from looping and burning money","updated_at":"2026-11-03T00:19:00Z","url":"https://agenttrafficcop.com"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"mgantlett"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["ai","agent"],"value":"Hi HN, Mark here.<p>If you've pointed Claude Code, Cursor, or OpenCode at a codebase you care about and walked away thinking <em>AI</em> coding tools are a net negative, I pretty much agree with you.<p>Left directly on a working branch, models optimize for shutting the compiler up on the current turn. Hit a tricky failure and they delete the failing assertion, return a hardcoded nil/true stub, rewrite the spec to match broken code, dump a 300-line function into the wrong package, or burn $15 of tokens editing in circles. Writing longer rules files (&quot;IMPORTANT: NEVER DELETE TESTS&quot;) breaks down once context fills up.<p>Starting over the summer I built Nomos: a single 28MB stripped static Go binary (CGO_ENABLED=0, Apache 2.0) that sits between your repo and whatever coding <em>agent</em> you already use. You run &quot;nomos init&quot; once and keep prompting in plain English - the <em>agent</em> drives the worktree, verification, and self-fix loop under the hood.<p>Instead of trusting prompts, Nomos locks main read-only via Git hooks, separates spec/contract tasks (INT-<i>) from code tasks (SUB-</i>) in a local causal DAG (nomos task dag) so an <em>agent</em> can't edit the spec to excuse broken code, and isolates every task in a Git worktree (worktrees/&lt;repo&gt;-&lt;task&gt;) under a strict state machine (IDLE -&gt; PLAN -&gt; EDIT -&gt; REVIEW -&gt; SYNC -&gt; LEARN). Throwing away a bad run is free.<p>Before anything merges to main, &quot;nomos verify&quot; runs 42 in-binary structural/AST gates (~460ms full pass, ~24ms single-file via --fast-file) plus your repo's own compiler and test suite. It blocks fake shims, deleted assertions, skipped tests (t.Skip, @pytest.mark.skip, #[ignore], @Disabled, [Fact(Skip=...)]), leaked secrets, functions over 60 LOC, complexity spikes, hardcoded paths, symlinks, and nomos.yaml rubric tampering against pinned main, returning file:line fixes for the next turn. Any gate failure or panic aborts the two-phase merge before main moves.<p>&quot;nomos mem&quot; stores architectural lessons locally in embedded pure-Go SQLite (mneme.db), promoting patterns only after a verified merge with a Git Notes receipt (refs/notes/nomos/releases) so failed runs don't poison memory. No cloud account, daemon, or telemetry.<p>One fun lesson from running the PreToolUse and PostToolUse hooks against the public TraceTrial-Bench harness benchmark (arXiv:2610.32285 - summary and spec in the repo README): my first PostToolUse hook injected helpful &quot;process failure / refusal&quot; warnings into tool results, and it degraded native OS EACCES guardrail attribution by -39.3% because synthetic hook text polluted error channels. Compiling &quot;nomos hook&quot; (session-start, pre-tool, post-tool at ~4ms cold start), keeping PostToolUse silent on native channels, and moving enforcement to PreToolUse path guards plus a 3-strike identical-failure circuit-breaker recovered that -39.3% drop and lifted stateful handoff, context continuity, and guardrail tasks cleanly.<p>Two rough edges upfront. First, while full type-checking, tests, and coverage ratchets (go test, LCOV, Cobertura XML) run via native toolchains across Go, TS/Svelte, Python, Rust, C/C++, Java/Kotlin, and C#, the fast in-binary pass uses go/ast for Go and combines a wazero WASM linear-memory cache and .scm query compiler with pure-Go multiline lexical scanners rather than bundling multi-MB per-language .wasm grammars (keeping the binary 28MB, CGO_ENABLED=0). Second, running the full IDLE -&gt; PLAN -&gt; EDIT -&gt; REVIEW -&gt; SYNC -&gt; LEARN loop adds turn overhead, so for a 100-line weekend prototype raw vibe-coding is faster - Nomos is for repos you still have to understand six months from now.<p>Curious how others here fence coding <em>agents</em> in on real repos (containers, Git hooks, jj workspaces, custom linters?), and what sneakier &quot;make the build green&quot; shortcuts still slip past your checks."},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"partial","matchedWords":["agent"],"value":"Show HN: Nomos \u2013 A Go harness that stops coding <em>agents</em> from cheating on tests"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://github.com/mgantlett/nomos"}},"_tags":["story","author_mgantlett","story_50337394","show_hn"],"author":"mgantlett","created_at":"2026-11-02T14:35:12Z","created_at_i":1793630112,"num_comments":0,"objectID":"50337394","points":3,"story_id":50337394,"story_text":"Hi HN, Mark here.<p>If you&#x27;ve pointed Claude Code, Cursor, or OpenCode at a codebase you care about and walked away thinking AI coding tools are a net negative, I pretty much agree with you.<p>Left directly on a working branch, models optimize for shutting the compiler up on the current turn. Hit a tricky failure and they delete the failing assertion, return a hardcoded nil&#x2F;true stub, rewrite the spec to match broken code, dump a 300-line function into the wrong package, or burn $15 of tokens editing in circles. Writing longer rules files (&quot;IMPORTANT: NEVER DELETE TESTS&quot;) breaks down once context fills up.<p>Starting over the summer I built Nomos: a single 28MB stripped static Go binary (CGO_ENABLED=0, Apache 2.0) that sits between your repo and whatever coding agent you already use. You run &quot;nomos init&quot; once and keep prompting in plain English - the agent drives the worktree, verification, and self-fix loop under the hood.<p>Instead of trusting prompts, Nomos locks main read-only via Git hooks, separates spec&#x2F;contract tasks (INT-<i>) from code tasks (SUB-</i>) in a local causal DAG (nomos task dag) so an agent can&#x27;t edit the spec to excuse broken code, and isolates every task in a Git worktree (worktrees&#x2F;&lt;repo&gt;-&lt;task&gt;) under a strict state machine (IDLE -&gt; PLAN -&gt; EDIT -&gt; REVIEW -&gt; SYNC -&gt; LEARN). Throwing away a bad run is free.<p>Before anything merges to main, &quot;nomos verify&quot; runs 42 in-binary structural&#x2F;AST gates (~460ms full pass, ~24ms single-file via --fast-file) plus your repo&#x27;s own compiler and test suite. It blocks fake shims, deleted assertions, skipped tests (t.Skip, @pytest.mark.skip, #[ignore], @Disabled, [Fact(Skip=...)]), leaked secrets, functions over 60 LOC, complexity spikes, hardcoded paths, symlinks, and nomos.yaml rubric tampering against pinned main, returning file:line fixes for the next turn. Any gate failure or panic aborts the two-phase merge before main moves.<p>&quot;nomos mem&quot; stores architectural lessons locally in embedded pure-Go SQLite (mneme.db), promoting patterns only after a verified merge with a Git Notes receipt (refs&#x2F;notes&#x2F;nomos&#x2F;releases) so failed runs don&#x27;t poison memory. No cloud account, daemon, or telemetry.<p>One fun lesson from running the PreToolUse and PostToolUse hooks against the public TraceTrial-Bench harness benchmark (arXiv:2610.32285 - summary and spec in the repo README): my first PostToolUse hook injected helpful &quot;process failure &#x2F; refusal&quot; warnings into tool results, and it degraded native OS EACCES guardrail attribution by -39.3% because synthetic hook text polluted error channels. Compiling &quot;nomos hook&quot; (session-start, pre-tool, post-tool at ~4ms cold start), keeping PostToolUse silent on native channels, and moving enforcement to PreToolUse path guards plus a 3-strike identical-failure circuit-breaker recovered that -39.3% drop and lifted stateful handoff, context continuity, and guardrail tasks cleanly.<p>Two rough edges upfront. First, while full type-checking, tests, and coverage ratchets (go test, LCOV, Cobertura XML) run via native toolchains across Go, TS&#x2F;Svelte, Python, Rust, C&#x2F;C++, Java&#x2F;Kotlin, and C#, the fast in-binary pass uses go&#x2F;ast for Go and combines a wazero WASM linear-memory cache and .scm query compiler with pure-Go multiline lexical scanners rather than bundling multi-MB per-language .wasm grammars (keeping the binary 28MB, CGO_ENABLED=0). Second, running the full IDLE -&gt; PLAN -&gt; EDIT -&gt; REVIEW -&gt; SYNC -&gt; LEARN loop adds turn overhead, so for a 100-line weekend prototype raw vibe-coding is faster - Nomos is for repos you still have to understand six months from now.<p>Curious how others here fence coding agents in on real repos (containers, Git hooks, jj workspaces, custom linters?), and what sneakier &quot;make the build green&quot; shortcuts still slip past your checks.","title":"Show HN: Nomos \u2013 A Go harness that stops coding agents from cheating on tests","updated_at":"2026-11-02T19:05:58Z","url":"https://github.com/mgantlett/nomos"}],"hitsPerPage":10,"nbHits":392,"nbPages":40,"page":0,"params":"query=AI+agent&tags=show_hn&hitsPerPage=10&page=0&numericFilters=created_at_i%3E%3D1791084783%2Ccreated_at_i%3C%3D1793676783&advancedSyntax=true&analyticsTags=backend","processingTimeMS":12,"processingTimingsMS":{"_request":{"queue":7,"roundTrip":21},"afterFetch":{"format":{"highlighting":1,"total":1}},"fetch":{"query":8,"scanning":2,"total":11},"total":12},"query":"AI agent","serverTimeMS":21}
